# DELIVER -- vibe86 v1 exception and interrupt delivery
# The core enters here (with the working registers rolled back to the
# faulting instruction's start) for every exception, INT n and INT3, with
# FAULT_VEC (vector; bit 8: has an error code), FAULT_ERR and FAULT_RIP set.
# It is the IA-32e-mode branch of INT n's Operation (Intel SDM Vol. 2A,
# "INT n/INTO/INT3/INT1", and Vol. 3A section 6.14 "Exception and Interrupt
# Handling in 64-bit Mode") for a 64-bit interrupt or trap gate at CPL 0:
# no stack switch, no IST, the stack aligned to 16 bytes, then SS, RSP,
# RFLAGS, CS, RIP and the error code pushed. Anything v1 can't deliver (an
# IDT too short, a gate not present) shuts the core down, as a triple fault
# would; so does a fault during delivery (the core checks that).
@operation
vec := FAULT_VEC AND FFH;
IF IDTR_LIMIT < SHL(vec, 4) + 15 THEN SHUTDOWN; FI;
gate := IDTR_BASE + SHL(vec, 4);
lo := Linear64[gate];
hi := Linear64[gate + 8];
IF (SHR(lo, 47) AND 1) = 0 THEN SHUTDOWN; FI;
target := (lo AND FFFFH) OR (SHR(lo, 32) AND FFFF0000H) OR SHL(hi, 32);
oldRSP := RSP;
oldFLAGS := RFLAGS;
RSP := RSP AND FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF0H;   (* 64-bit mode; legacy-mode delivery: TODO *)
Push(SS);
Push(oldRSP);
Push(oldFLAGS);
Push(CS);
Push(FAULT_RIP);
IF (FAULT_VEC AND 100H) != 0 THEN Push(FAULT_ERR); FI;
IF (SHR(lo, 40) AND 0FH) = 0EH THEN SetFlag(INTF, 0); FI;
SetFlag(TF, 0);
SetFlag(NT, 0);
SetFlag(RF, 0);
RIP := target;
@flags
None.
